# 11. Product Copy

Product copy should make the food desirable, but it should also explain why the product belongs in the Charter system.

The product is not the whole story. It is where the evidence becomes tangible.

Use sensory language sparingly and precisely. Rich, savoury, warming, clean, deep, properly made and carefully selected are useful. Avoid indulgent luxury language unless the food genuinely warrants it.

Product copy should connect at least one of these ideas:

1. Whole animal value.
2. Better economics for farmers.
3. A named farming system or cohort.
4. Living Certificate proof.
5. Nutritional or eating quality logic.
6. Traceability from field to pack.

Example:

Bone Broth is made from beef bones simmered slowly over many hours, creating a rich stock with depth of flavour and natural collagen. It is also an expression of whole animal value, turning parts of the animal too often overlooked into food with purpose, nutrition and economic value for the farmer.

